THE CODE IS MIT. THE DICTIONARY IS NOT, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS.

This licence covers the software in this repository. It does not and cannot
relicense the language data the software is built on, which arrives under
terms set by the people who made it:

  Estonian forms and example sentences
      From Ekilex, the lexicographic database of the Institute of the
      Estonian Language (Eesti Keele Instituut). Licensed CC BY 4.0.
      Attribution required. https://ekilex.ee

  English glosses
      From English Wiktionary, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 by its contributors.
      Attribution required, and share-alike: a work built on this text has
      to be offered on the same terms. https://en.wiktionary.org

  Word frequency counts
      From hermitdave/FrequencyWords over the OpenSubtitles corpus. MIT for
      the code, CC BY-SA 4.0 for the counts: attribution required, and
      share-alike. `lib/collections/frequency.ts` is built from them and
      carries the same terms. https://github.com/hermitdave/FrequencyWords

  Every spelling of every word
      `prisma/data/forms/` is the forms list: whether a spelling is Estonian
      and which headword it belongs to, and nothing else. Built by
      `scripts/build-forms.ts` from three sources. The Ekilex enumeration
      above; Ekilex's own inflection tables as published in
      KristjanPikhof/Estonian-Wordlist-Enriched-Ekilex, CC BY 4.0 for the
      Institute's data and CC BY-SA 4.0 for that repository, so the list
      carries share-alike; and Vabamorf, Filosoft's open-source
      morphological analyser and synthesiser for Estonian, LGPL, whose
      lexicon the synthesised forms come from.
      https://github.com/KristjanPikhof/Estonian-Wordlist-Enriched-Ekilex
      https://github.com/Filosoft/vabamorf

  Which word a spelling is
      `prisma/data/homographs.json` is Vabamorf's reading, LGPL, of the
      words in the dictionary's own sentences: which spellings belong to
      more than one word, and which word each sentence means. Built by
      `scripts/build-homographs.ts`. It decides only which sentences a word
      may borrow, and holds no form, gloss or sentence of its own.
      https://github.com/Filosoft/vabamorf

  Synthesised speech
      From TartuNLP at the University of Tartu, fetched at runtime and
      cached. Not redistributed as part of this repository.
      https://tartunlp.ai

  Hearing Estonian
      The speech model is TalTech's streaming Zipformer for Estonian,
      licensed MIT by the Laboratory of Language Technology at Tallinn
      University of Technology. Attribution required. It runs in sherpa-onnx,
      licensed Apache 2.0, whose WebAssembly build `npm run asr:runtime`
      vendors into public/asr/runtime and whose notice travels with it.
      Neither is committed: the model is fetched by `npm run asr:fetch` and
      served from the deployment's own storage. TalTech's pair judge, which
      this app pins and does not yet load, is CC BY-SA 4.0.
      https://huggingface.co/TalTechNLP/streaming-zipformer.et-en
      https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx

  Typefaces
      `app/fonts/` holds Onest and Schibsted Grotesk as served by Google
      Fonts, one file per subset. Both are under the SIL Open Font License
      1.1, whose text sits beside them (OFL-Onest.txt and
      OFL-SchibstedGrotesk.txt). They are committed so a build never has to
      download them. https://github.com/googlefonts/onest
      https://github.com/schibsted/schibsted-grotesk

`prisma/data/expanded.json` is a build product of the first two and
`lib/collections/frequency.ts` of the third, so the share-alike clause reaches
both: they may be redistributed, and a redistribution carries CC BY-SA 4.0
rather than this licence. Nothing about that is a restriction on the code
around it.

The University of Tartu publishes a better Estonian frequency dictionary and
it is CC BY-NC. It is deliberately not used: no charge today is not a promise
of no charge ever, and a non-commercial clause is the one licence a project
cannot walk itself back out of later.

Every source above whose terms ask for a credit is credited in the running
app, on the sign-in page, in the landing page footer and on /terms, which is
where a licence of this shape expects to find them rather than in a file only
a developer opens. `scripts/test-invariants.ts` reads this list and fails on a
source one of those three does not name.
